{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-175589",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's swap the left and right-hand sides so that the variable is on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n-\\frac{m}{3}=\\frac{1}{15}\n$$\n\nThen, we remove the negative sign from the variable. To do this, we multiply both sides of the equation by $-1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{m}{3} & =\\frac{1}{15} \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-\\frac{m}{3}) & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{15} \\\\ \\frac{m}{3} & =-\\frac{1}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the equation above, $m$ is being divided by $3.$ To isolate $m$, we can perform the opposite operation, which is *multiplying* by $3.$ So, we multiply both sides of the equation by $3$ and simplify.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{m}{3} & =-\\frac{1}{15} \\\\ 3\\cdot \\frac{m}{3} & =3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{15}) \\\\ \\frac{3m}{3} & =-\\frac{3}{15} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{3}m}{\\cancel{3}} & =-\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ m & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
